1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 Alright, let's talk about how to convert from ODE kind of representation to transfer function representation.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 Remember, the transfer function really is just in the class space.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 It's the thing that if you multiply it by the input, you get the output.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 It's kind of the mapping from input to output.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 Which means that getting the transfer function from an ODE is actually a really easy process.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 All you have to do is kind of take the Laplace transform of both sides of your ODE,

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 one side being kind of a function of the outputs and one side kind of being a function of the inputs.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
 And then rearrange the results so that it looks like output equals something times input.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
 This should always be possible with ODEs, at least the kinds of ODEs that you want to be looking for transfer functions for anyway.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 Remember, transfer functions are kind of defined for linear systems and linear systems that have zero initial conditions.

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 Those are kind of the two key facts.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
 If you find that you've tried to take kind of the Laplace transform of an ODE to get a transfer function,

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 you can't actually get a result.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 You're either doing something wrong or kind of a subset of doing something wrong.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 You're trying to find the transfer function of a system that it's not defined for a system which doesn't have zero initial conditions

16
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 or a system which isn't linear.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
 A quick fact that will help you on your quest to convert ODEs to transfer functions.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 If you want to find the Laplace transform of the derivative of a thing, say the derivative of the function, why?

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 Well, that's going to be kind of S times the Laplace transform of y.

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 There's also a term that has to do with initial conditions, but in the context of transfer functions, remember, zero initial conditions.

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 So those terms kind of disappear.

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 And the same for every derivative after that.

23
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
 So a second derivative of y becomes a S squared kind of big y, a third derivative S cubed big y, and so on.

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 If you have derivatives of u, it's the same kind of deal.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 If you have kind of the first derivative of u, it's S times big u, the second derivative of u, it's S squared times big u,

26
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,000
 and so on.

27
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 Let's have a go at problem 7.1 together and see if we can convert some ODEs to transfer functions.
